60,3,1,talk,Asuka,,"Option B is the most logical. It removes the ""ownership"" of spots and encourages a more fluid use of the space.","{""words"":21,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,1,talk,Ryuji,,"Option A provides a revenue stream, but as I've seen in the ledger, the administrative burden of billing might outweigh the gain.","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,1,talk,George,,Option C addresses the fairness issue directly. It ensures that no one is permanently disadvantaged by the current layout.,"{""words"":19,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,1,talk,Shunichi,,Option A is a disaster waiting to happen. I've already seen how fees kill the community spirit in the next block.,"{""words"":21,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Mio,,"Option B sounds like a lot of physical work to move bikes, and those racks are hard to use! Option C is just a drawing!","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,2,talk,Asuka,,"@Mio, the ""work"" of moving bikes is already happening in a chaotic way. Option B formalizes the shared nature of the space.","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[""Mio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,Ryuji,,Option C is the most administratively lean. It requires one meeting a year and no ongoing billing or ledger entries.,"{""words"":20,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,George,,"@Ryuji, I agree. A lottery is a clean break from the past five years of stagnation. It's fair and transparent.","{""words"":20,""mentions"":[""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Shunichi,,"A lottery is a gamble, but it's a one-time event. It's better than a monthly bill that makes people resent their neighbors.","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Mio,,"Exactly! Option C is the easiest! No monthly paperwork, no greasing racks, just one big party once a year to pick names!","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,3,talk,Asuka,,"While Option C is simple, it doesn't solve the physical congestion. If the ""bad"" spots are still bad, the lottery just rotates the misery.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,Ryuji,,"The ""misery"" of a bad spot is preferable to the financial instability of a poorly managed fee system. I lean toward Option C.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,3,talk,George,,The physical layout is a secondary concern to the social friction caused by perceived favoritism. Option C solves the social problem.,"{""words"":21,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,3,talk,Shunichi,,@George is right. The physical layout is a nuisance; the social friction is a poison. Let's go with the lottery and keep the peace.,"{""words"":24,""mentions"":[""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,3,talk,Mio,,I'm totally for the lottery! It's the only one that doesn't sound like a second job for the treasurer.,"{""words"":19,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
75,3,4,talk,Asuka,,"I still believe Option B is the only one that addresses the actual morning bottleneck, but I see the consensus forming.","{""words"":21,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
76,3,4,talk,Ryuji,,Reason dictates that the most sustainable solution is the one with the lowest administrative and financial risk. That is Option C.,"{""words"":21,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
77,3,4,talk,George,,I am satisfied with Option C. It is a fair way to reset the system and remove the grumbling about tenure.,"{""words"":21,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
78,3,4,talk,Shunichi,,It's the least likely to turn the lobby into a cold zone. I'm in.,"{""words"":14,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
79,3,4,talk,Mio,,"Yay! Let's do the lottery! It's fun, it's fair, and it's easy-peasy!","{""words"":12,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
